  "modules": [
    {
      "name": "Current",
      "source": "Src/Current.rux",
      "header": "What a thread can ask about itself and the machine it runs on."
    },
    { "name": "Error", "source": "Src/Error.rux", "header": "Why a thread could not be started." },
    {
      "name": "Native",
      "source": "Src/Native.rux",
      "header": "How each system starts, joins and detaches a thread, and what it says about the current one.\nLinux, FreeBSD and macOS start threads through the C library's `pthread_create`, which gives the thread its stack and\nits thread-local block; a thread the kernel started directly would have neither. Windows starts them with\n`CreateThread`, whose handle is joined by waiting on it. Both kinds are kept in one word, so the rest of the package\nnever asks which system it runs on."
    },
    {
      "name": "Spawn",
      "source": "Src/Spawn.rux",
      "header": "Starting a thread, and the handle that joins it or lets it go.\nA thread runs a function on a context value the caller moves in, and leaves the function's result for whoever joins\nit. Rux has no closures, so the function and its context travel separately and meet on the new thread.\nThe thread and its handle share one block: a header, the function, the context the thread takes, and the result it\nleaves. Both own the block, and the header counts them. Whichever lets go last releases it, and destroys a result\nnobody took, so a handle dropped while its thread still runs is safe: the thread finishes, finds itself last, and\ncleans up. Joining takes the result first, then lets go."
    }
  ],
